WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.230 --> 00:00:08.395
This episode is sponsored by Endel, an app that creates
 personalized soundscapes to help you focus, relax and sleep.

00:00:08.395 --> 00:00:14.034
The first 100 people to click on the link in the 
description will get a free week of audio experiences.

00:00:32.798 --> 00:00:36.230
Every experiment has to start somewhere.

00:00:36.230 --> 00:00:42.780
This one began in a container full of dying
microbes, and the five cute, pink ciliates

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:48.030
called blepharisma that James—our master
of microscopes—carefully transferred to

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:50.670
a new flask for rescue.

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:55.420
Within that flask was clean water and a few
grains of wheat.

00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:58.940
But those grains were not there to feed the
blepharisma.

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:05.260
They were there to feed the other hidden residents
of the flask, the bacteria, expanding their

00:01:05.260 --> 00:01:08.910
numbers to create a buffet for the blepharisma
to eat.

00:01:08.910 --> 00:01:15.380
And in their new home with its plentiful fields
of bacteria to graze on, the blepharisma thrived.

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:22.690
They divided—and sometimes multiple times
per day—so that five became 10, became 20,

00:01:22.690 --> 00:01:25.330
became 40, and so on.

00:01:25.330 --> 00:01:34.750
After only 25 rounds of division, those 5
cells had become more than 150 million iterations

00:01:34.750 --> 00:01:36.177
of themselves.

00:01:36.177 --> 00:01:42.550
And in just two weeks, what was a fresh flask
full of clear water had become tinged with

00:01:42.550 --> 00:01:49.340
flecks of red, pigmented with the thriving
population of blepharisma descended from just

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:53.070
a handful of survivors.

00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:58.290
If microbes like the blepharisma can make
so many of themselves in such a short amount

00:01:58.290 --> 00:02:05.370
of time, why is our planet not just completely
coated in any single species?

00:02:05.370 --> 00:02:11.677
Why don’t we see this reddish water everywhere,
chock full of blepharisma?

00:02:11.677 --> 00:02:18.250
Well for one, the flask these blepharisma reside
in is an idealized world, with endless nutrients

00:02:18.250 --> 00:02:23.929
and countless bacteria—not to mention an
intervening master of microscopes.

00:02:23.929 --> 00:02:27.730
But the world outside of this flask is far
less simple.

00:02:27.730 --> 00:02:34.099
There are so many organisms to compete with,
all trying to make a life out of the space

00:02:34.099 --> 00:02:37.670
and food and oxygen available to them.

00:02:37.670 --> 00:02:43.709
And those resources are constantly in flux,
heightening the stakes as organisms contend

00:02:43.709 --> 00:02:46.644
for the possibility of future shortages.

00:02:47.276 --> 00:02:51.069
The problem for blepharisma is not just
the intricacies of ecology.

00:02:51.069 --> 00:02:53.049
They face another threat.

00:02:53.049 --> 00:02:57.189
And it is not a threat that you or I or even
a myriad of other organisms on this earth

00:02:57.189 --> 00:02:59.000
could relate to.

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:03.822
We’re talking, of course, about giant cannibals.

00:03:06.109 --> 00:03:11.329
The cannibals in this flask did not make their
appearance known until James began to reduce

00:03:11.329 --> 00:03:17.170
the amount of food he added to this culture
of blepharisma, which makes sense.

00:03:17.170 --> 00:03:20.580
Cannibalism doesn’t necessarily make for
a good survival strategy.

00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:24.780
When your species is attempting to make more
of itself, taking the time to chow down on

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:27.749
each other just seems counter-productive.

00:03:27.749 --> 00:03:32.769
And yet, there are various ciliates that have
been observed eating their own kind.

00:03:32.769 --> 00:03:38.420
For example, our favorite blue trumpets, Stentor
coeruleus, have been documented tearing chunks

00:03:38.420 --> 00:03:44.436
off other Stentor coeruleus-es, grabbing them
by the tail and swallowing them1.

00:03:44.436 --> 00:03:49.905
While we haven’t been able to record a successful
cannibalistic Stentor, we have seen them try

00:03:49.905 --> 00:03:52.965
—like this one, attempting to get its 
mouth around the other.

00:03:53.215 --> 00:03:57.790
In this case, the targeted individual was
able to get away, probably because it was

00:03:57.790 --> 00:04:00.760
too big for the other Stentor to actually
fit into its mouth.

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:06.349
There’s a lesson in there for ciliates pursuing
the cannibalistic lifestyle: it’s not enough

00:04:06.349 --> 00:04:10.659
to be vicious, you also need to be big.

00:04:10.659 --> 00:04:14.340
Ciliates do not have forks and knives to carve
up their plate of food.

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:16.940
They don’t even have limbs.

00:04:16.940 --> 00:04:23.340
What most ciliates do have is an oral groove,
an opening lined with hair to stir up a vortex

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:25.220
and draw food in.

00:04:25.220 --> 00:04:29.560
So whatever the food they’re eating, ciliates
have only a few options.

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:33.500
They can jab away at their prey, tearing chunks
off in the process.

00:04:33.500 --> 00:04:37.750
Or they can engulf it in one piece.

00:04:37.750 --> 00:04:43.860
And for most blepharisma, peacefully minding
their own business with bacteria meals, that’s fine.

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:47.160
But cannibalism demands more.

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:52.000
The blepharisma needs to be big enough to
eat other blepharisma.

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:59.433
And that’s why cannibalistic blepharisma
also tend to be giant blepharisma.

00:04:59.433 --> 00:05:05.220
Ciliate cannibalism is not very well understood,
and so the exact relationship between size

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:07.230
and cannibalism is murky.

00:05:07.230 --> 00:05:12.987
Are the blepharisma cannibals because they are
giants, or are they giants because they’re cannibals?

00:05:12.987 --> 00:05:15.260
In nature, maybe it’s a bit of both.

00:05:15.260 --> 00:05:21.090
But in the lab, well, scientists have realized
that there’s a more intriguing possibility,

00:05:21.090 --> 00:05:24.000
and our own blepharisma have shown this.

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:29.741
It’s the possibility that you can actually
intentionally turn your jolly pink ciliates

00:05:29.741 --> 00:05:31.253
into cannibals.

00:05:31.253 --> 00:05:36.460
Like we said at the beginning, every experiment
has to start somewhere, and in the 1930s,

00:05:36.460 --> 00:05:43.591
the scientist Arthur C. Giese started with
a group of blepharisma that had grown up on bacteria.

00:05:43.591 --> 00:05:49.177
These blepharisma were content with their
smaller meals, and cannibalism was rare.

00:05:49.177 --> 00:05:54.950
But Giese hypothesized that this peace was
a conditional one, that resulted simply from

00:05:54.950 --> 00:06:00.790
the fact that the blepharisma he was looking
at were all too close in size for any individual

00:06:00.790 --> 00:06:02.870
to pursue cannibalism.

00:06:02.870 --> 00:06:06.831
But what if one could make a larger blepharisma?

00:06:06.831 --> 00:06:12.543
That is what Giese decided to find out, and
his method was pretty simple: to get a bigger

00:06:12.543 --> 00:06:15.780
blepharisma, feed them bigger food.

00:06:15.780 --> 00:06:20.250
The blepharisma he was working with weren’t
large enough yet to eat each other, but they

00:06:20.250 --> 00:06:27.550
could eat a ciliate called Tetrahymena that
was smaller than them but larger than bacteria.

00:06:27.550 --> 00:06:30.490
After eating the Tetrahymena, the blepharisma
grew.

00:06:30.490 --> 00:06:35.930
Their mouths were larger, and even their macronucleus
had expanded to almost three times the size

00:06:35.930 --> 00:06:38.310
as their bacteria-fed counterparts.

00:06:38.310 --> 00:06:43.360
Then, starved of their Tetrahymena diets,
the giant blepharisma were set upon a batch

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:48.450
of blepharisma that were small enough to stuff
in their microbial gullet.

00:06:48.807 --> 00:06:52.640
The smaller blepharisma, but did not stand
a chance.

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:58.960
Within minutes, most of the giant blepharisma
Giese observed had eaten at least one Blepharisma.

00:06:58.960 --> 00:07:04.800
Eight hours later, he found one giant had
eaten 12!

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:09.620
And while they are quick to be captured, the
ingested blepharisma’s death is slow.

00:07:09.620 --> 00:07:14.370
The cannibal might take around 30 seconds
to engulf their prey, but Giese reported seeing

00:07:14.370 --> 00:07:21.620
the captured blepharismas swimming laps inside
food vacuoles for up to an hour, after which

00:07:21.620 --> 00:07:24.990
the digestion began to kick in.

00:07:24.990 --> 00:07:28.930
The cannibalism Giese observed was not permanent
though.

00:07:28.930 --> 00:07:34.216
Eventually, after being starved, the giant
cannibals he had cultivated divided several

00:07:34.216 --> 00:07:40.985
times, creating smaller versions of themselves
in the process, bringing his experiment full circle.

00:07:40.985 --> 00:07:46.440
Small blepharisma sustaining larger blepharisma
who will eventually return to their small

00:07:46.440 --> 00:07:51.510
size…until the call for cannibalism strikes
again.

00:07:51.510 --> 00:07:55.980
The blepharisma in our flask grew up differently
than Giese’s did.

00:07:55.980 --> 00:08:00.130
They weren’t separated into two batches
and fed different diets that would eventually

00:08:00.130 --> 00:08:04.610
drive the physical differences that would
make one group cannibalistic.

00:08:04.610 --> 00:08:09.690
Yet there were still blepharisma that grew
into giants capable of cannibalism.

00:08:09.690 --> 00:08:15.600
And we don’t know the mechanisms that spurred
those changes, whether there was something

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:20.580
in these organisms’ pasts that predisposed
some of them towards gigantism.

00:08:20.580 --> 00:08:27.330
Nor do we know what their futures look like,
and whether it will be full of cannibalism.

00:08:27.330 --> 00:08:31.470
Every experiment has to start somewhere.

00:08:31.470 --> 00:08:37.579
But by the end, it is only a snapshot of a
moment in time, one that captures the effects

00:08:37.579 --> 00:08:47.399
of many mysterious histories, and the beginning
of many unknown futures.

00:08:47.399 --> 00:08:53.339
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

